    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay argues that immediate interventions in public health crises -- as opposed to long-term interventions -- aren't just short term fixes. Instead, the author contends that immediate interventions are not only easier to implement than long term interventions, but they also initiate a virtuous cycle that improves both the economic and societal well-being of a region, a positive feedback loop between improved health and increased socioeconomic development could reduce or even eliminate the need for foreign aid.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
